README-VI -- seven files, three editors, and which one to takeIf you are copying part of this collection onto your own media, you want ONEvi.  This file is here so you do not have to install several and try them.Short answer: take `elvis' unless you have a reason not to.  It needs noMicroware module, it is the only one with its manual on the disk, its wholesource is here, and it brings a read-only `view' and a few helpers with it.Take `vi' instead if you specifically want ex mode or the genuine Berkeleybehaviour; take `vi_nocio' if you are short of memory and want the plainestthing that edits.THE SEVEN FILES ARE THREE EDITORS---------------------------------  file                  bytes  needs   module   source      docs                                cio     name  ---------------------------------------------------------------------  vi                    91760   yes     vi      69 files    1 (German)  vi_cio                61706   yes     vi      -           -  vi_nocio              81464   no      vi_nocio -          -  REBUILT/VI            78488   no      vi      -           -  elvis                111944   no      elvis   65 files    14 files  view                  15132   no      view    with elvis  with elvis  REBUILT/vi.elvis      15130   no      vi      with elvis  with elvis  Berkeley ex/vi   vi  PVIC             vi_cio, vi_nocio, REBUILT/VI       three builds of one  Elvis 1.7        elvis, view, REBUILT/vi.elvis      one program, three namesYOU CANNOT KEEP SEVERAL AND CHOOSE PER COMMAND----------------------------------------------Four of the seven carry the MODULE name `vi': the Berkeley one, vi_cio,REBUILT/VI and vi.elvis.  OS-9 finds a program by module name once it isresident, whatever path you type, so whichever of those loads first answersfor all of them afterwards.Measured 2026-08-30: `/dd/CMDS/REBUILT/VI' opens a file and fills the screen.Do `load /dd/CMDS/vi', then run that same path again, and you get the othereditor instead.  Nothing warns you.This is the practical reason to pick one and copy only that one.vi -- the genuine article-------------------------  Version 1.0, 01/90, ported for OS-9/68k by Helmut Hoheisel-Zimmermann and  published on EFFO forum disk 13.  Not a clone: its source in SRC/effo_vi is  the Berkeley ex/vi source itself -- ex_vmain.c, ex_vops.c, ex_re.c and the  rest, 69 files.  If you want the editor's exact behaviour down to the  corners, this is the one that has it by construction.  41 `:set' options, and alone here in supporting `lisp', `redraw' and  `hardtabs'.  It changes personality by flag, as the original did:      vi -x file      becomes ex      vi -d file      becomes edit  Costs: it needs Microware's `cio' (it is starred in DOC/INDEX).  Its only  documentation here is DOC/vi/vi.doc, which is in German.  Its companions  `expreserve' and `exrecover' save and restore work if the editor is killed.  One unexplained caveat: under this collection's capture harness -- a  pseudo-terminal rendered as vt100 -- it paints one line and a half-drawn  rule and no more.  On a real terminal it works fine and is in daily use.  Tried and did not explain it: 24 lines and 36, TERMCAP as a path and as an  entry, ^L and ^R.  Treat it as a fact about the harness.elvis -- the one with everything around it------------------------------------------  Elvis 1.7 by Steve Kirkendall, ported by Peter Reinig, built here from  CMDS/archives/elvis1.7.lzh.  A clone: very close, not identical.  58 `:set' options, the most of the three, and the only one here with  colour and attribute handling (`charattr'), digraphs and a configurable  `equalprg'.  It arrives as a family:      view              elvis opened read-only      ref               look a C function up from a tags file      fmt               format text      elvprsv, elvrec   preserve and recover a session across a crash      REBUILT/vi.elvis, input.elvis, ctags.elvis  `view' and `vi.elvis' are small wrappers that exec `elvis', so they need  CMDS/elvis present.  If you copy elvis, copy those you want with it.  Costs: the largest of the three, and a clone rather than the original.  Best documented by a wide margin -- DOC/elvis holds the full man page set,  and SRC/elvis the whole 65-file source.PVIC -- the small plain one---------------------------  PVIC 1.0a, Portable VI Clone, public domain.  Three builds are here:  `vi_nocio' and `REBUILT/VI' need no Microware module, `vi_cio' does.  `REBUILT/VI' is under that name because `vi' was taken.  The plainest of the three: the core vi command set and the common options  (autoindent, ignorecase, showmatch, tabstop) without wrapmargin,  shiftwidth, paragraphs or sections.  Costs: no source and no documentation on this disk -- it came as a binary,  and its own `About PVIC' screen is what there is.CHOOSING--------  You want a manual to read when something misbehaves    elvis  You want ex mode, or exact Berkeley behaviour          vi  You are short of memory, or want the plainest          vi_nocio  You have no Microware cio and do not want to need one  elvis or vi_nocio  You want read-only browsing                            view (needs elvis)  Any of them edits this disk's CR-terminated text correctly; all three are  OS-9 native and none of them will mangle a line ending.  If you take exactly one file and nothing else: `elvis'.